---
import type { ImageMetadata } from "astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Opening from "../components/Opening.astro";
import Tour from "../components/Tour.astro";
import Feature from "../components/Feature.astro";
import Frame from "../components/Frame.astro";
import KeyList from "../components/KeyList.astro";
import Aboard from "../components/Aboard.astro";
import Install from "../components/Install.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const files = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>("../assets/shots/*.png", { eager: true });
const shot = (name: string) => {
  const img = files[`../assets/shots/${name}.png`]?.default;
  if (!img) throw new Error(`missing screenshot: ${name}.png`);
  return img;
};
---

<Base
  title="Safina: the Kubernetes terminal UI for teams that ship"
  description="The Kubernetes terminal UI for teams that ship, built for the people on call. Find what is broken, see why, fix it without leaving the keyboard, and keep the record for the morning."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <Opening />
    <Tour />

    <Feature id="claude" name="Ask Claude" heading="It already knows what you’re looking at.">
      <p>
        Press <kbd class="cap">i</kbd> and ask what’s wrong. Claude already has the cluster, namespace and resource
        you have selected and what you have already tried on it, and <kbd class="cap">c</kbd> in the Logbook hands
        it the logs you read. Nothing to paste, nothing to explain&nbsp;twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its counsel comes as keys: it tells you which safina keys to press next but can’t press them, and anything
        that writes still asks you first. The <i>amanah</i>, the trust of the cluster, stays&nbsp;with&nbsp;you.
      </p>
      <p class="quiet">Uses the <code>claude</code> CLI you are signed in to. No API&nbsp;key&nbsp;needed.</p>
      <Frame
        slot="figure"
        src={shot("chat")}
        crop={shot("chat-m")}
        panCols={86}
        alt="The chat tab beside the workloads list. With the ledger-api logs attached from the Logbook, Claude explains that the pod panics on startup because the name ledger-db-primary does not resolve, warns not to press R because a restart would crash the same way, and lists the keys to check next: / to filter for ledger-db, e for events, d to find where the host is set, U to roll back."
      />
    </Feature>

    <Feature id="safety" name="Barbarossa mode" heading="Lowercase reads. Uppercase writes.">
      <p>
        Every key that changes the cluster is a capital letter, so a slip of the finger can only read. Hold Shift
        and you are in <b>Barbarossa mode</b>, after the red-&#8288;bearded Ottoman admiral: the keys that write are red,
        and so is every confirmation. Scaling asks for a count, restarts and rollbacks ask for a yes, edits show
        the diff&nbsp;first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then <b>Rollout watch</b> takes over. A scale or restart isn’t done when the API accepts it, so safina
        follows it until the pods are ready, and tells you if they never&nbsp;get&nbsp;there.
      </p>
      <p class="quiet">
        <b>No autopilot</b>, either: safina never sets a course for you. Every session starts on the clusters in
        your kubeconfig, and you pick&nbsp;one.
      </p>
      <Frame
        slot="figure"
        src={shot("diff-f")}
        crop={shot("diff-m")}
        panCols={78}
        alt="An edit to the ledger-api Deployment waiting for confirmation in a red frame: a one-line diff changes DATABASE_URL from ledger-db-primary to ledger-db, with a red y to apply and n to cancel."
      />
      <div slot="after" class="keys">
        <KeyList />
      </div>
    </Feature>

    <Feature id="logbook" name="Logbook" heading="Still there in the morning.">
      <p>
        Kubernetes keeps events for an hour, and a replaced pod takes its logs with it. The <b>Logbook</b> keeps
        what you looked at: the logs and events you read, every write with the state before and after it, and when
        each&nbsp;happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open it from <kbd class="cap">:</kbd>. When the storm has passed, export the night as a timeline with a
        postmortem template, no cluster needed, and read it back in calm water, or hand it to Claude to
        piece&nbsp;together. Every entry keeps its <i>isnad</i>, its chain: which cluster, which session, when it
        happened and when you&nbsp;saw&nbsp;it.
      </p>
      <p class="quiet">
        Kept on your machine, readable only by you, with secrets redacted. It records what you did and saw in
        safina; it is not an audit of the&nbsp;cluster.
      </p>
      <Frame
        slot="figure"
        src={shot("history")}
        crop={shot("history-m")}
        panCols={80}
        alt="The Logbook for payments-api, newest first: a restart and, before it, a scale to 3 replicas, each step timestamped from requested, through the before snapshot, the API accepting it and the after snapshot, to progress and completed at 3 of 3 ready."
      />
    </Feature>

    <Aboard />
    <Install />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .keys {
    margin-top: var(--s4);
  }
</style>
